<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.6.1: Using Linear Functions to Describe Constant Speed</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01830</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.6.1: Using Linear Functions to Describe Constant Speed</md:title>
    <md:uuid>9d54fc92-7a19-48bb-847c-68e5ab6fadb9</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>A cannon is 10 feet off the ground. It launches a cannonball straight up with a velocity of 406 feet per second.</para>
<para>Imagine that there is no gravity and that the cannonball continues to travel upward with the same velocity.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Answer questions a–f using the table with the heights of the cannonball at different times.</item>
</list>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        Seconds
      </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        Distance above ground (feet)
      </emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        0
      </entry>
      <entry>
        10
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry>
        a. _____
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        b. _____
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry>
        c. _____
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
      <entry>
        d. _____
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        5
      </entry>
      <entry>
        e. _____
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(t\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        f. _____
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<section class="check-understanding"><title>Warm Up Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="3a577da6-6105-4e46-85c7-cb4e068e4bfe"><problem><para>
      <para> How far above the ground is the cannonball at 1 second?</para>
        <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>416</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is: The cannonball is 416 feet above the ground at 1 second.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> How far above the ground is the cannonball at \(t\) seconds?</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The cannonball is \(10+406t\) feet above the ground at \(t\) seconds.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write an equation to model the distance in feet, \(d\), of the ball \(t\) seconds after it was fired from the cannon, if there was no gravity.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(d=10+406t\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content>
</document>
